{
  "image_name": "2bbe6e8314743503e1ee832c77e81089.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment around bladder cancer cells and links specific immune or stromal cell types to prognosis and therapy response. Cancer cells sit at the center and are influenced by several surrounding populations that either weaken anti-tumor immunity or, in one case, support it.\n\nOn the suppressive side, regulatory T cells marked by FOXP3 and PDL1 are associated with recurrence after BCG therapy and appear to reduce T cells and dendritic cells while releasing inhibitory factors such as TGFβ and IL-10. Tumor-associated macrophages expressing Gal-9 and DC-SIGN are tied to poor prognosis and seem to decrease dendritic cells while producing inflammatory or suppressive cytokines. Dendritic cells labeled CD83 are associated with poor response to BCG and reduced recurrence-free survival, with the figure emphasizing impaired T-cell activation and antigen presentation. Cancer-associated fibroblasts marked FAP are linked to higher-grade disease and lower survival, apparently promoting EMT. The tumor also recruits MDSCs through CCL2, and these cells are associated with reduced therapeutic response.\n\nIn contrast, CD8-positive tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes are associated with increased patient survival. Red inhibitory lines indicate that suppressive myeloid populations and the tumor environment limit these beneficial CD8 T cells.",
